{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hasbajrami\",\"court\":\"Second Circuit\",\"date\":\"2019-12-18\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"945 F.3d 641\",\"proposition\":\"Querying a Section 702 database with a U.S.-person identifier is a distinct Fourth Amendment event whose reasonableness must be assessed separately from the initial incidental collection.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"FISA-702-querying\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"Section 702 backdoor search Fourth Amendment circuit\\\" / Second Circuit opinion and recent Hasbajrami remand commentary\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Leaders of a Beautiful Struggle v. Baltimore Police Department\",\"court\":\"Fourth Circuit en banc\",\"date\":\"2021-06-24\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"2 F.4th 330\",\"proposition\":\"Baltimore's persistent aerial-surveillance program effected a search because its retrospective citywide imagery could reveal the whole of a person's movements.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"persistent-aerial-surveillance\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"persistent aerial surveillance Carpenter recent commentary\\\" / Fourth Circuit en banc opinion and Chatrie commentary\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Tuggle\",\"court\":\"Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2021-07-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"4 F.4th 505\",\"proposition\":\"Eighteen months of warrantless pole-camera observation of a home's publicly visible exterior was not a search, although a ubiquitous camera network coupled with powerful analytics could present a different case.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole-camera-mosaic\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"pole camera circuit Tuggle House Carpenter\\\" / Seventh Circuit opinions and 2024–2025 pole-camera cases\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Moore-Bush\",\"court\":\"First Circuit en banc\",\"date\":\"2022-06-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"36 F.4th 320\",\"proposition\":\"A fractured en banc court affirmed the denial of suppression on good-faith grounds without a controlling merits rationale, while five judges concluded that eight months of pole-camera surveillance was a Carpenter search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole-camera-mosaic\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"Moore-Bush recent pole camera circuit split\\\" / First Circuit en banc opinion and Green commentary\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"People v. Seymour\",\"court\":\"Colorado Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2023-10-16\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"536 P.3d 1260\",\"proposition\":\"A reverse-keyword warrant implicates protected privacy and expressive interests and lacked individualized probable cause, although the evidence was admitted under the good-faith exception.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"reverse-keyword-warrant\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"reverse keyword warrant appellate Seymour recent\\\" / Colorado Supreme Court opinion and 2025–2026 reverse-search commentary\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Ortiz-Orellana\",\"court\":\"Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-01-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"90 F.4th 689\",\"proposition\":\"Historical CSLI obtained before Carpenter under a Stored Communications Act Section 2703(d) order remained admissible because officers reasonably relied on then-valid statutory and appellate authority.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"CSLI-good-faith\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 circuit Carpenter CSLI good faith\\\" / Fourth Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Whipple\",\"court\":\"Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-02-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-5126\",\"proposition\":\"A subpoena for one Walmart Pay transaction and associated subscriber information fell within the third-party doctrine because the records were voluntarily disclosed conventional business records rather than automatically generated location data.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"third-party-doctrine\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 circuit third party doctrine digital records Carpenter\\\" / published Sixth Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Zelaya-Veliz\",\"court\":\"Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-02-16\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"94 F.4th 321\",\"proposition\":\"Social-media warrants may use a two-step process that obtains broad account data but limits seizure to evidence of enumerated crimes, while a warrant lacking any temporal limit raises serious particularity concerns yet qualified for Leon good faith.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"social-media-warrant-particularity\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 social media warrant temporal limit circuit\\\" / Fourth Circuit opinion, Justia, and certiorari petition\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hay\",\"court\":\"Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-03-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"95 F.4th 1304\",\"proposition\":\"Sixty-eight days of warrantless pole-camera surveillance directed at publicly exposed portions of a home was not a search, and Carpenter's mosaic reasoning did not extend to that conventional visual surveillance.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole-camera-surveillance\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 federal circuit pole camera Hay\\\" / Tenth Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Mapson\",\"court\":\"Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-03-21\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"96 F.4th 1323\",\"proposition\":\"The court avoided deciding whether police access to an automated-license-plate-reader database was a search because then-binding pre-Carpenter location-data precedent supplied objective good faith.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"ALPR-good-faith\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 circuit automated license plate reader Fourth Amendment Mapson\\\" / Eleventh Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Briscoe\",\"court\":\"Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"101 F.4th 282\",\"proposition\":\"A Maryland probable-cause tracking order was the functional equivalent of a warrant authorizing use of a cell-site simulator and was adequately supported by probable cause.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"cell-site-simulator\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 circuit cell site simulator warrant Briscoe\\\" / Fourth Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Mendez\",\"court\":\"Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-06-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"103 F.4th 1302\",\"proposition\":\"A brief manual inspection of a phone's photo gallery at the international border was a routine border search requiring neither a warrant nor individualized suspicion.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"border-device-search\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 circuit border phone search Mendez\\\" / Seventh Circuit opinion and Eta discussion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie\",\"court\":\"Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"107 F.4th 319\",\"proposition\":\"The panel held that acquiring two hours of voluntarily enabled Google Location History through a geofence warrant was not a search, a ruling later vacated for en banc rehearing.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence-warrant\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 geofence warrant circuit Chatrie\\\" / Fourth Circuit panel opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Nkongho\",\"court\":\"Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"107 F.4th 373\",\"proposition\":\"Border agents could seize devices for a contemplated forensic search when individualized suspicion connected the search to border purposes, and the nine-day interval before seeking a warrant was reasonable.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"border-device-forensics\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 Fourth Circuit border forensic device Nkongho\\\" / Fourth Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Davis\",\"court\":\"Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"109 F.4th 1320\",\"proposition\":\"The defendant could not challenge geofence data associated with another person's Google account and device because he failed to establish a personal reasonable expectation of privacy in the searched data.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence-standing\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 Eleventh Circuit geofence Davis\\\" / Eleventh Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Karmo\",\"court\":\"Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-31\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"109 F.4th 991\",\"proposition\":\"A credible threat of armed looting and shooting created exigent circumstances for an emergency request for real-time CSLI without a warrant.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"real-time-CSLI-exigency\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 circuit real-time CSLI exigent circumstances Karmo\\\" / Seventh Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Smith\",\"court\":\"Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"110 F.4th 817\",\"proposition\":\"Obtaining Google geofence data was a search and the multistage warrant process was a categorically unconstitutional general warrant, although suppression was denied under the good-faith exception.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence-warrant\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 Fifth Circuit geofence Smith general warrant\\\" / Fifth Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie\",\"court\":\"Fourth Circuit en banc\",\"date\":\"2024-11-01\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"The court granted rehearing en banc and vacated the panel opinion that had treated acquisition of Google Location History as outside the Fourth Amendment.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence-warrant-en-banc-grant\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"Chatrie en banc grant November 2024\\\" / Fourth Circuit rehearing order\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. House\",\"court\":\"Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-11-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-1950\",\"proposition\":\"Thirteen months of warrantless pole-camera surveillance of publicly visible areas around a home was not a search under Tuggle because the cameras provided prospective conventional observation rather than retrospective comprehensive tracking.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole-camera-surveillance\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2024 Seventh Circuit pole camera House Tuggle\\\" / published Seventh Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Gregory\",\"court\":\"Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-02-13\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"128 F.4th 1228\",\"proposition\":\"Long-term continuous pole-camera recording of areas exposed to public view was not a search despite the duration and persistence of the surveillance.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole-camera-surveillance\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2025 Eleventh Circuit pole camera Gregory\\\" / Eleventh Circuit opinion and later circuit citations\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Harry\",\"court\":\"Second Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-03-07\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"130 F.4th 342\",\"proposition\":\"Fifty days of continuous pole-camera monitoring of a business exterior and adjacent public areas was conventional visual surveillance rather than a Carpenter-style comprehensive search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole-camera-surveillance\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2025 Second Circuit pole camera Harry\\\" / Second Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Pulido\",\"court\":\"Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-04-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"133 F.4th 1256\",\"proposition\":\"A forensic search of an electronic device at the international border requires neither a warrant nor individualized suspicion under binding circuit precedent.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"border-device-forensics\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2025 Eleventh Circuit forensic border device Pulido\\\" / Eleventh Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Age\",\"court\":\"Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-04-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"136 F.4th 193\",\"proposition\":\"Pre-Carpenter historical CSLI obtained through Section 2703(d) orders was admissible under statutory good faith even though a nonbinding district-court decision had questioned the statute's constitutionality.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"CSLI-good-faith\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2025 Fifth Circuit CSLI Carpenter Age\\\" / Fifth Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie\",\"court\":\"Fourth Circuit en banc\",\"date\":\"2025-04-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"136 F.4th 100\",\"proposition\":\"An equally divided en banc court affirmed the suppression denial without a controlling merits rationale because sufficient judges found no search or relied on good faith, leaving the geofence question unresolved in the circuit.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence-warrant\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2025 en banc Chatrie 136 F.4th 100\\\" / Fourth Circuit en banc opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"The Heidi Group, Inc. v. Texas Health & Human Services Commission\",\"court\":\"Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-05-28\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"138 F.4th 920\",\"proposition\":\"A government investigator conducted a search by inducing a former employee to use retained credentials to access a company's private Dropbox, and cloud storage did not trigger the third-party doctrine because the contents had not been exposed to the provider or public.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"cloud-storage-third-party-doctrine\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2025 Fifth Circuit Dropbox Fourth Amendment Heidi Group\\\" / Fifth Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Green\",\"court\":\"D.C. Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-08-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"149 F.4th 733\",\"proposition\":\"Pole-camera observation of a home's publicly visible rear area was not a search on the record presented, although the court declined to hold that prolonged camera surveillance can never create a mosaic problem.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"pole-camera-mosaic\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2025 D.C. Circuit pole camera Green mosaic\\\" / D.C. Circuit opinion and Justia\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Armendariz v. City of Colorado Springs\",\"court\":\"Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-03-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1201\",\"proposition\":\"Warrants for protesters' devices, expansive keyword-responsive data, and all Facebook content were plausibly and clearly overbroad because they lacked a crime-specific nexus and failed to distinguish searchable data from seizable evidence.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"digital-warrant-particularity\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"24-1201 digital warrants protest keyword Tenth Circuit\\\" / revised published Tenth Circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Porter\",\"court\":\"Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-03-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"170 F.4th 381\",\"proposition\":\"Periodic license-plate detections from a limited public-road ALPR network were not sufficiently comprehensive to constitute a Carpenter search, and an outstanding-warrant alert furnished reasonable suspicion.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"ALPR-surveillance\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2026 Fifth Circuit ALPR Porter Fourth Amendment\\\" / Fifth Circuit opinion and reporter listing\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Tew\",\"court\":\"Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-04-06\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1333\",\"proposition\":\"A warrant authorizing years of email, messages, photos, location information, and application data from an Apple cloud account was insufficiently particular, although Leon good faith prevented suppression.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"cloud-account-warrant-particularity\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2026 Tenth Circuit Apple account warrant Tew\\\" / published Tenth Circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Chatrie v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2026-06-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-112; 609 U.S. ___\",\"proposition\":\"Government acquisition of a person's Google Location History through a geofence demand is a Fourth Amendment search even when the period is brief and the records are held by a third party, requiring renewed analysis of probable cause and particularity at each stage.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"geofence-warrant\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"Supreme Court Chatrie geofence opinion 25-112\\\" / official Supreme Court opinion PDF\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Eta\",\"court\":\"Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-07-06\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-1891\",\"proposition\":\"Several hours of manual phone inspection at the border remained a routine suspicionless border search, and advance FBI coordination or an ordinary-crime investigative motive did not invalidate the objectively authorized search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"border-search-parallel-construction\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2026 Seventh Circuit Eta border phone FBI coordination\\\" / published Seventh Circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Cardozo\",\"court\":\"Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-07-13\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-4239\",\"proposition\":\"A manual phone inspection at the international border was a routine border search requiring no individualized suspicion, distinct from a later forensic examination.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"border-device-search\",\"discovery_path\":\"\\\"2026 Fourth Circuit Cardozo manual phone border search\\\" / published Fourth Circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"}}
